  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Family: Amphiliidae. Size: 6 cm. Temperature: 18–22°C. pH: 6.5–7.5.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>This species, and probably most of the genus, is easily misidentified. The thumbnail images may show this species or a closely related one such as A. platychir or A. uranoscopus. They are all more or less the same when kept in aquaria. They are not imported very often but would make good aquarium additions if kept in optimum conditions.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Africa: Gbin River, Guinea, and Pra River, from Sierra Leone to Ghana. Type locality: Atesu River, Gold Coast. It also occurs in Liberia and the Ivory Coast. The species is widely distributed from the St. John (Liberia) to the Mono river (Togo), and in the upper Niger basin.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>They inhabit flowing waters in their natural habitat.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Can be kept in aquaria with a small-grained gravel or sand substrate, well planted, with a slight current to mimic their natural habitat. Provide rockwork and caves. Water should be kept on the cool side.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Temperature: 18–22°C. pH: 6.5–7.5.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Mosquito larvae, Tubifex, Cyclops, Daphnia, grindal worms, vegetable food, tablet and flake.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: the specific name atesuensis: –ensis, suffix denoting place: Atesu River, Ghana, type locality. IUCN Red List: no known widespread threats, assessed as Least Concern (IUCN 2020). Remarks: the last two thumbnail images were photographed in the Côte d'Ivoire Tai National Park in the south west of Côte d'Ivoire, close to the border with Liberia.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Baensch, H.A. and R. Riehl 1991 Aquarien atlas. Bd. 3. Melle: Mergus, Verlag für Natur- und Heimtierkunde, Germany. 1104 p.</p><p>Dankwa, H. &amp; Olaosebikan, B.D. 2020. Amphilius atesuensis. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2020.</p><p>Ferraris, C.J. Jr., 2007. Checklist of catfishes, recent and fossil (Osteichthyes: Siluriformes), and catalogue of siluriform primary types. Zootaxa 1418:1-628.</p><p>The ETYFish Project Fish Name Etymology Database © Christopher Scharpf.</p>"
    }
  ],
